# Copy to `.env` (compose reads it automatically from this directory) and fill in.
#
# NOTHING SECRET GOES HERE. This file is the boring, checked-in configuration: hostnames, identifiers, paths.
# Secrets (DATABASE_URL, FORGE_VAULT_KEY, the seed token) are fetched by env-source.sh and exported into the
# shell, so they never land on disk. Shell values win over this file — that is what makes the split work.
#
# ★ THE VALUES BELOW ARE THE BENCH'S, not a deployment's. This instance runs on a laptop today; the lines
# marked ⚠️ are the ones that change the day it gets a hostname.

# --- ⚠️ how this box is reached ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On the bench: one origin, plain HTTP, one port. The admin is served under /admin* (see caddy/Caddyfile.local).
# In a deployment: the real hostnames, FORGE_CADDYFILE unset (the production edge is the default), and the
# admin gets a subdomain of its own — an admin sharing an origin with the shop shares its cookies too.
FORGE_CADDYFILE=./caddy/Caddyfile.local
# ★★★ WHICH INTERFACE THIS BOX PUBLISHES ITS DOORS ON, AND THE BOX HAS TO SAY (pk24/d4).
#
# ⛔ EVERY DOOR OF THIS BENCH IS PLAIN HTTP, AND THE FRONTS RUN `NODE_ENV=production`, so every cookie they
# set is `Secure` — and a browser silently refuses a `Secure` cookie on any plain-http origin that is not
# `localhost`. Published on every interface, `http://<tailnet-name>:8200` answers 200 and the shopper's cart
# evaporates; `:8201` shows the admin login, takes the password, and bounces back to /login with an empty
# jar. Both silent. Measured on this box (`bin/box-up.sh` carries the browser half; the four doors answering
# 200 from off the box was re-measured 2026-09-08), and it is the same defect `pk23/p5` closed on the
# product's `:3033`.
#
# ★ `127.0.0.1` IS THE BENCH'S ANSWER AND IT COSTS THE BENCH NOTHING. `localhost` is a secure context, so
# every address in the README keeps working over plain http; and `tailscale serve` terminates TLS and
# proxies to `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`, so the tailnet keeps every https door it had. What stops answering
# is exactly the address that was lying.
#
# ⚠️ EMPTY IS A LEGAL ANSWER AND IT MEANS "EVERY INTERFACE" — that is what a deployment says, and it is
# byte-for-byte what this box published before this line existed. What is NOT legal is saying nothing:
# compose interpolates `${FORGE_BENCH_BIND?…}` (no colon), so an absent variable stops `docker compose` by
# name before the first container. A LAN device with no tailnet is a real reason to set it empty — read the
# paragraph above first, because the trap comes back with it.
FORGE_BENCH_BIND=127.0.0.1
FORGE_HTTP_PORT=8200
FORGE_HTTPS_PORT=8243
# ── ★★★ THE SIX FACES OF THIS BOX, ONE VARIABLE EACH (pk34/d1) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A deployment of this instance answers on SIX hostnames — three shops, two admins and the counter. WHICH
# hostname each face gets is NOT decided here: `seed/box.json` declares one `domain` per store and one
# `admin_domain` per tenant (a hostname is DATA — it is the `host` column the kernel keys its directory on),
# and each of those declarations names the variable below that carries it to the edge. `caddy/Caddyfile`
# decides the other half — which CONTAINER answers there — and `bin/box-domains.guard.mjs` grades the pairing
# in both directions, plus that compose really delivers each variable to the edge container.
#
# ⚠️ A BENCH NAMES NONE OF THEM AND NOTHING CHANGES. The box is born on `localhost` (§0b) and the promotion
# is a NAMED step; the bench opts into `caddy/Caddyfile.local` on the line above, which knows nothing about
# hostnames. `FORGE_DOMAIN`/`FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN` keep `localhost` because containers other than the edge read
# them; the four below stay EMPTY here.
#
# ⇒ ★★★ AND A DEPLOYMENT DOES NOT TYPE THEM EITHER (pk35/d4). `bash bin/box-up.sh --promote <one of those
# hostnames>` writes all six FROM `seed/box.json`, which is where they are declared — a hostname is DATA, and
# a value typed here beside a file that already states it agrees only until somebody renames one. Promoting
# to an address the declaration does NOT name (a tailnet, a laptop) leaves these alone and says so: those
# hostnames are not addresses that box answers at. And a promotion that would leave one of the six on its
# sentinel while writing the others REFUSES, naming the variable — see `bin/promotion-faces.mjs`.
#
# ⛔ AND AN EMPTY ONE DOES NOT KILL THE BOX ANY MORE, WHICH IT USED TO. `caddy/Caddyfile` had a
# `{$FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN}` site block and compose never passed that variable, so it resolved EMPTY — and an
# empty site address is not a missing host, it is a file that does not parse: «server block without any key
# is global configuration», i.e. store, checkout and admin down together, measured against the real file on
# 2026-09-12. Every address now carries a `<something>.unset.localhost` sentinel (in BOTH compose and the
# Caddyfile — Caddy's own fallback does not fire for a variable that is present and EMPTY, measured), and
# `.localhost` is issued by Caddy's internal CA with no ACME request at all. So a forgotten variable costs
# ONE face on a name nothing resolves, and `bin/verify-config.mjs` names it at the end of the birth.
FORGE_DOMAIN=localhost
FORGE_OUTLET_DOMAIN=
FORGE_CAFE_DOMAIN=
FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN=localhost
FORGE_CAFE_ADMIN_DOMAIN=
# The address a BROWSER reaches this stack at. It is not decoration: the kernel's local media driver derives
# every product image URL from it, so a wrong value gives a catalogue of broken images and nothing in a log.
FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8200

# --- who this instance is ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ★★ THIS BENCH HAS **TWO TENANTS**, and the values below are the FIRST one's. `provision-ref` reads them at
# bootstrap and creates a tenant + its FIRST store; the second tenant is a SECOND RUN of the same one-shot
# with these three overridden on the command line. See README, "The bench", for the exact two commands.
#
#   T1  forgeco    · stores `forge` (bootstrap) + `outlet`   · admin on :8201
#   T2  forgecafe  · stores `cafe`  (bootstrap) + `balcao`   · admin on :8202
#
# The stores that are NOT the bootstrap one are created through the port by `bin/seed-box.mjs`, from
# `seed/box.json`, because a second store is data and not bootstrap.
FORGE_REF_TENANT=forgeco
FORGE_REF_STORE_HANDLE=forge
FORGE_REF_STORE_NAME=Forge

# ★★ EMPTY ON PURPOSE — THIS IS WHAT LETS ONE ADMIN CONTAINER SERVE BOTH TENANTS.
#
# With a value here the admin is PINNED: it manages that one tenant and never looks at the request's Host.
# Empty puts it in HOST mode, where it asks the kernel which tenant the hostname belongs to
# (`read.admin.by_host`) and mints a login-driver for it from FORGE_ADMIN_PLATFORM_TOKEN. The lookup keys on
# `host:port` first, which is why the two admins differ only by port on this bench.
#
# ⚠️ Setting this again silently collapses the bench to one tenant: the second admin port would answer, and
# serve the pinned tenant, which is the least diagnosable shape this can take.
#
# ⛔ AND IT IS ALSO WHAT DECIDES WHICH GATE DOOR EXISTS. `/enter` reads the PER-TENANT map
# (`FORGE_ADMIN_ACCESS_KEYS`, below) in host mode; the singular pair a pinned instance uses names no tenant,
# so a box that fills this in has no per-hostname door at all — every "abrir o admin" lands on a login form.
FORGE_ADMIN_TENANT=

# The first operator of EACH tenant, invited by the bootstrap one-shot. Without it nobody can enter that
# tenant's admin. This is the address the OTP is sent to, so it must be a mailbox you can actually read.
#
# ★ AND IT MUST BE A GOOGLE WORKSPACE ADDRESS, not merely a reachable one: the demo's default operator
# signs in with GOOGLE, so the account has to be one the provider knows. An address that only receives mail
# gets you in by OTP and leaves the OIDC path — which the demo exists to show — untested and unusable.
#
# ⛔ THE VALUE BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER, and it is one on purpose: this file is versioned and source-available,
# so a real mailbox written here would be a private address shipped to everyone who reads the repository.
# Put YOUR OWN Workspace address in `.env`; a box born from the placeholder mails an account nobody reads and
# nobody can enter the admin.
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=operator@example.com
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_NAME=Marina Alves

# ★★★ THE BENCH'S OWN MAILBOX — the one fact that decides where EVERY message of this box goes.
#
# Set (any value) → the `mailpit` service of compose.yml is created and the four `FORGE_SMTP_*` are pointed
# at it by `env-source.sh`. Nothing leaves this machine, every login code is readable by anyone with the box,
# and the Resend key — if this box has one — is not used.
# EMPTY → the real provider, which is what a DEPLOYMENT wants. It is SET here because this file is the
# BENCH's configuration (the same seam that decides the port numbers and the bind interface), and an instance
# starts from a `.env` of its own. The distinction is declared, never inherited: the collector's service
# carries a compose PROFILE, so a box that says nothing does not merely fail to use it — it never creates it.
#
# ★ AND IT REMOVES A HAZARD THIS BOX ALREADY HAD. The demo dataset carries real addresses; a bench on the
# real provider can mail actual people from fictional orders, which is why the buyer's order mail had to be
# seeded OFF. With the collector there is no outside to reach.
#
# ⛔ WHY A BENCH NEEDS IT AT ALL: the transport that prints the code to a terminal is constructible only under
# `!production` and this box runs `NODE_ENV=production`, so a box without a mailbox does NOT log the code —
# every message fails by name and nobody can log in. Until 2026-09-13 the only deliverable address on either
# bench was a single personal mailbox, so no one else could sign in as anybody. See mail/README.md.
FORGE_BENCH_MAILBOX=1

# --- our own apps -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `demo-gate` is the only one, and it is here rather than on composition.json because an app of ONE box is
# MOUNTED, never composed into an image.
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_DIR=/app/extensions
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_HOST_DIR=./extensions

# --- the themes -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where BOTH fronts look for a theme folder — a store's `theme_key` names one of them (./themes/outlet,
# ./themes/coffee-store). Mounted into the storefront AND the checkout: a themes mount on the vitrine alone
# gives a shop that changes clothes at the "Finalizar compra" button.
FORGE_THEMES_DIR=/app/themes-instance
FORGE_THEMES_HOST_DIR=./themes

# --- media ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMPTY on purpose FOR THE KERNEL. With no base URL its local driver derives one from FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN,
# which is exactly what a bench wants. Point a bucket/CDN base here the day the demo needs one.
#
# ⚠️ THE FRONTS DO NOT SHARE THAT DEFAULT, and compose gives them one of their own (see DIFFERENCE 4 there).
# They join base+key server-side for the DERIVATIVE door (`/api/img`), which is how a composed banner is
# served — and with no base that door answers `404 media base not configured`, so every tile of a banner
# mosaic is a broken image while the product photos beside it are fine. Setting a value here overrides all
# three, which is what a real bucket wants.
FORGE_MEDIA_BASE_URL=

# --- the gate ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The demo-gate app's own wiring, read when its block renders. Both optional.
#
# ⚠️ THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "THE GATE'S SCREEN DOES NOT RENDER YET", and it had been false since Forge P1:
# an app of ONE box may now be on `instanceApps` in `composition.json`, the oven composes it, and both front
# registries are built from that list — so the interstitial DOES render, on the reference storefront, the one
# the Outlet runs unforked. See README §4.
#
# ★ AND THESE ARE STILL ONLY TWO, WHICH SINCE pk35 IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A GAP. The gate's HUB layout —
# one card per tenant, over six destinations — SHIPPED, and it needed no variable at all: the six addresses are
# DATA this box already declares once, at the top of this file and in `seed/box.json`, and the app reads them
# through `apps/demo-gate/faces.generated.ts` (rendered by `bin/gate-faces.mjs`, drift-checked by
# `bin/gate-faces.guard.mjs`). Four more FORGE_GATE_* variables would have been a second spelling of the six
# above, and a second spelling is the thing that rots.
#
# `FORGE_GATE_ADMIN_URLS` keeps ONE job, and it is the one a declaration cannot do: it is each tenant's admin
# as this box REALLY answers today — `{"<tenant id>": "<origin>"}`, written by `bin/box-up.sh` (step 3d at
# birth, from `seed/box.json`'s `admin_host`; the promotion rewrites it from the doors the admin directory
# accepted) — so a bench and a tailnet get a working admin link while the declared hostname is something
# nobody has published yet. It wins over the declaration for the faces it names and for no other.
#
# ⚠️ IT USED TO BE SINGULAR, AND THAT WAS THE THIRD EXEMPLAR OF ONE VALUE FOR A PLURAL THING. The hub draws
# one admin row per tenant and this box has two; a single origin could only be handed to ONE card, so one
# brand's row carried the working door and the other carried a hostname nothing resolves. Keyed by tenant id,
# a third brand declared in `seed/box.json` arrives here with no second edit.
FORGE_GATE_SITE_URL=https://forgecommerce.pro
FORGE_GATE_ADMIN_URLS=

# ── THE GATE'S `/enter` DOOR — THE NON-SECRET HALF (pk38/d8) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# `{"<tenant id>": "<store id>"}`. The admin's `/enter` route trades a redeemable key for a signed-in session
# on the kernel's PUBLIC redeem face, which takes no credential — so a STORE is what fixes which tenant the
# key is checked against, and it has to be a store of that same tenant.
#
# ⚠️ EMPTY HERE AND NOT HAND-WRITTEN, for the same reason as `FORGE_STORE_HOSTS` above: the ids are fresh
# ULIDs minted at every birth, so a map carried over from another box names stores that do not exist.
# `bin/box-up.sh` (step 3b) writes it from what `provision-ref` just returned.
#
# ★ WHAT THE ADMIN ACTUALLY READS IS THE MERGE OF THIS AND THE KEYS, and no container reads this line:
#
#     FORGE_ADMIN_ACCESS_KEYS={"<tenant id>":{"store":"<store id>","key":"<redeemable key>"}}
#
# The keys are SECRETS, so they live in the secret store and `env-source.sh` assembles the map from both
# halves. ⛔ A key never goes into a file beside this one: `.env` is read by compose and by `source`, and a
# key in it is a key in every scrollback that ever prints the file.
#
# ★★★ AN ENTRY IS WHOLE OR ABSENT. A tenant missing either half is dropped from the merged map entirely and
# its admin falls through to its own login screen — it can NEVER inherit the other brand's key, which is what
# would sign a visitor on one hostname into another tenant.
FORGE_ADMIN_STORE_IDS=

# --- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared between admin, storefront and kernel so a write busts the right cache entry. Any random string.
FORGE_REVALIDATE_SECRET=
# host → store override, only useful while cutting over. Normally empty: host → store is DATA (set the store's
# public URL in the admin and it routes, no restart).
#
# ⚠️ ON THIS BOX IT IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT HAND-WRITTEN. `bin/box-up.sh` (step 3b) REWRITES this line
# at every birth from the store id `provision-ref` just returned — the ids are fresh ULIDs, so a map carried
# over from another box points at stores that do not exist. Leave it empty here.
FORGE_STORE_HOSTS=

# ── THE ADMIN'S SIBLING SWITCHER (A44) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The dropdown that carries an operator from one brand's admin to the other's: `[{ "name": …, "url": … }]`,
# one entry per tenant, read by the admin at BOOT.
#
# ⚠️ EMPTY HERE ON PURPOSE, AND FOR THE SAME REASON AS THE LINE ABOVE — `bin/box-up.sh` (step 3d) DERIVES it
# from `seed/box.json` (`settings.tenant_name` + `admin_host`) and writes it. That is what this variable is
# doing in a versioned file at all: it used to be typed by hand on the bench and every rebirth erased it, and
# an erased switcher is invisible — an unset/invalid value yields an EMPTY LIST and the admin shell renders
# exactly as it did before the feature existed. A third tenant in `seed/box.json` arrives here by itself.
#
# ★ SWITCHING BRANDS MEANS LOGGING IN AGAIN, and that is correct rather than missing. Each admin is its own
# host and the session cookie is host-only; the re-login is what stops an operator from acting on one tenant
# while believing they are on the other. It is not SSO and is not trying to be.
FORGE_ADMIN_SIBLINGS=

# ── WHAT ONLY EXISTS ONLINE, AND DIES IN A RESET (pk10·D1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A RESET MUST TURN BACK ON EVERYTHING THAT ONLY EXISTS ONLINE — a CDN the demo leans on, and anything else
# of that species, which a reset destroys and no local step recreates.
#
# The facilities themselves are DECLARED in `seed/box.json` (`online_only`), which is what `bin/online-only.mjs`
# answers for and what step 15's configuration verdict reports. These two variables SELECT the driver for
# each, and both are EMPTY here because the bench has neither: caddy caches nothing, and the media is a
# docker volume that `bin/box-down.sh` destroys by name.
#
# ⚠️ NAMING A DRIVER THIS REPOSITORY DOES NOT IMPLEMENT REFUSES THE RUN, on purpose. A step that quietly did
# nothing when configured would fail on exactly the box the setting exists for — which is the shape of every
# defect this box's install rehearsal turned up. Empty ⇒ an announced no-op; a name ⇒ run it or refuse.
FORGE_EDGE_PURGE_DRIVER=
FORGE_MEDIA_STORE_DRIVER=

# ── THIS BOX AT AN ADDRESS — THE PROMOTION, NOT THE BIRTH (A15, pk24/§B5) ──────────────────────────────────
# The bench is born on `localhost`. When it has to be reachable from a phone or another machine:
#
#     bash bin/box-up.sh --promote tailnet          (and `--promote localhost` to undo it)
#     bash bin/box-up.sh --promote demo.example.com any address this box really answers at
#     bash bin/box-up.sh --tailnet | --localhost    the two aliases, kept
#
# ★ THE TAILNET IS ONE DESTINATION, NOT THE DEFINITION. The two variables below are what a TAILNET
# destination needs; a public hostname is named on the command line instead and needs neither.
#
# That step rebuilds the host → store map, re-points FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN and FORGE_GATE_ADMIN_URLS, re-derives
# FORGE_ADMIN_SIBLINGS and claims each tenant's admin hostname THROUGH THE PORT. It is idempotent, and it
# never CONFIGURES `tailscale` — getting the machine onto the network is the operator's gesture; this only
# wires the box to the fact that it is.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE PORT IT CLAIMS IS THE ONE `tailscale serve` PUBLISHES, NOT THE ONE THIS BOX LISTENS ON. Those
# are different numbers whenever `serve` is doing the publishing — it terminates TLS on ports of its own —
# and getting it wrong is silent in BOTH directions: over http the admin's `Secure` session cookie is never
# stored (so the login "works" and the next click bounces to /login) and over https the directory has no
# claim for the address (so the login refuses with `unknown_admin_host`). So the promotion READS
# `tailscale serve status --json` and derives every address from it. Nothing published, or no `tailscale`
# on the box: it falls back to the direct ports below, which is what it always did.
#
# ⚠️ BOTH VALUES ARE THE OPERATOR'S OWN PRIVATE NETWORK AND STAY OUT OF GIT. `.env` is gitignored; this file
# is not, so it names the variables and never a value. Leave them empty for a laptop-only bench.
#
#   FORGE_TAILNET_HOST   the MagicDNS name this machine answers to. Required by the promotion.
#   FORGE_TAILNET_IP     its tailnet address. Optional — the safety net for a device on the tailnet whose
#                        MagicDNS is off, which resolves the name to nothing.
FORGE_TAILNET_HOST=
FORGE_TAILNET_IP=
# Set to 1 to enable /login/ops (paste a raw token) as a break-glass door into the admin.
FORGE_ADMIN_OPS_LOGIN=
FORGE_TLS_CERT_DIR=./caddy/certs
# ── THE EDGE'S TWO EXTENSION FOLDERS, AND THEY ARE TWO ON PURPOSE (A10) ──────────────────────────────────
# `caddy/extra/`       is read by `caddy/Caddyfile` at TOP level → every file there is a SITE BLOCK.
# `caddy/extra-local/` is read by `caddy/Caddyfile.local` from INSIDE its `:80` site → FRAGMENTS (`handle`).
# One folder, one reader: both globs used to point at `caddy/extra/` on the theory that `*.local.caddy` was
# narrower than `*.caddy`. It is a strict SUBSET, so production parsed the bench's fragment as a site address
# and REFUSED THE WHOLE FILE — store, checkout and admin down together, invisible on the bench.
# `bin/caddy-extra-door.guard.mjs` is the rule; caddy/extra-local/README.md is the story.
FORGE_CADDY_EXTRA_DIR=./caddy/extra
FORGE_CADDY_EXTRA_LOCAL_DIR=./caddy/extra-local
FORGE_ADMIN_I18N_HOST_DIR=./i18n

# --- the port on the internet -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO MAY REACH THE CONTROL FACE (/v1/control/*), as space-separated CIDRs. EMPTY IS THE ANSWER here: the
# Caddyfile's own default is loopback, i.e. nobody. (The bench edge does not route it at all.)
FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR=
FORGE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_CREDENTIAL=
FORGE_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=

# The admin gets its own origin on the bench (a path prefix does not work — caddy/Caddyfile.local
# carries the measurement). In a deployment the admin has FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN and this is inert.
#
# ★ TWO PORTS, ONE CONTAINER, TWO TENANTS. `read.admin.by_host` resolves the tenant from `host:port`, so on a
# bench where every door is `localhost` the PORT is the only thing telling one brand's admin from the other's.
# `:8201` → forgeco, `:8202` → forgecafe. The map is DATA, claimed at bootstrap via FORGE_ADMIN_HOST.
FORGE_ADMIN_HTTP_PORT=8201
FORGE_ADMIN2_HTTP_PORT=8202

# The bench mailbox's web face — where a human reads the login code (and every other message this box sends)
# when FORGE_BENCH_MAILBOX is set. Published on FORGE_BENCH_BIND like every other door here; the collector's
# SMTP port is deliberately NOT published, because the kernel reaches it by service name.
FORGE_MAIL_HTTP_PORT=8204

# ── THE COFFEE FORK'S OWN STORE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The café's storefront is a FORK of ours (`storefront-coffee/`), and a fork MAY name a store of its own —
# the reference vitrine may not, because an entry there would be one customer's id inside every instance's
# image. This is what names it: the store whose institutional pages render in the CAFÉ'S words instead of the
# body shared with the reference storefront (`storefront-coffee/src/templates/cms/registry.ts`).
#
# ⛔ IT CANNOT BE TYPED HERE AND MEANT, and that is the whole reason this paragraph exists. A store id is a
# ULID minted by `provision-ref` on EVERY birth, so an id copied from another box matches nothing — silently,
# because the page still answers 200 in somebody else's words. `caddy/extra-local/coffee.caddy` shipped with
# exactly that and fooled two people; `bin/box-up.sh` step 3c now GENERATES both, from the id it just
# provisioned. So this line is a sentinel and step 3c overwrites it.
#
# ⚠️ SAME SENTINEL AS THE COUNTER'S, AND NOT AN EMPTY STRING — see the paragraph below for the measurement.
# (Here compose reads it soft, so an empty value would not kill the box; it is spelled this way so that a
# human reading `.env` can tell "not born yet" from "born and this is the id", which an empty line cannot.)
FORGE_COFFEE_STORE_ID=sto_PENDING_SEED

# ── THE COUNTER'S TOTEM (T2·S2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The sixth image of this instance: one Next app of ours serving the whole counter host. Build it with
# `bash bin/build-totem.sh <forge checkout>` before `docker compose up`.
#
# ★★ THE STORE TAKES TWO VARIABLES, AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANCY. The public read face resolves a store by ID
# or by HOST; there is no `store.by_handle` (the only capability that returns a handle lives on the internal
# face, behind the admin service token, which the totem deliberately does not hold). So:
#   · FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID     — what the process actually sends to the port. Required.
#   · FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_HANDLE — what a HUMAN reads to know which shop that id is. Required, and the reason
#                                the id above is reviewable at all.
# The app refuses to boot if either is missing, or if the handle was pasted into the id slot.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE ID SHIPS AS `sto_PENDING_SEED`, NOT EMPTY, BECAUSE EMPTY KILLS A VIRGIN BOX AT STEP 1.
# Measured 2026-09-05 with `docker compose config`: `${FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID:?…}` (compose.override.yml)
# refuses a variable that is PRESENT AND BLANK exactly as it refuses one that is absent —
# *"required variable FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID is missing a value"*. The compose interpolates the whole file on
# every command, so `cp .env.example .env && bash bin/box-up.sh` died on step 1 complaining about the
# COUNTER, whose store id only becomes knowable at step 6. The first thing a new operator does was the one
# thing that could not work, and the error named a screen they had not reached yet.
#
# `sto_PENDING_SEED` is not a placeholder this file invented: `bin/box-up.sh:1322` already reads it as
# "the counter has no store yet, so do not start the totem and SAY SO", and step 6 rewrites this line with
# the real id once it provisions the store. The sentinel existed and nothing wrote it — this writes it.
FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID=sto_PENDING_SEED
FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_HANDLE=balcao
# How long the screen waits before deciding the customer walked away: it clears the basket and returns to
# "Toque para começar". Seconds. Lower it to rehearse the reset; do not raise it much — the whole point is
# that one person's order never greets the next person.
# ⚠️ It does NOT run while a pix is waiting to be paid. Once the order is placed the QR is the only copy of
# what can settle it, and stillness there means "paying in the bank app", not "gone" — so the pix's own
# window (900s, the kernel's reservation) is what frees the till instead. See totem/src/components/Totem.tsx.
FORGE_TOTEM_IDLE_SECONDS=90
# The bench port for the totem, published by compose.override.yml (site `:82` in caddy/Caddyfile.local),
# mirroring the admin's own port. Unused in a deployment, where the counter gets the hostname below.
FORGE_TOTEM_HTTP_PORT=8203
# The counter's public hostname in a DEPLOYMENT (caddy/Caddyfile) — one of the SIX faces declared at the top
# of this file; `seed/box.json` names which store it belongs to. Leave empty on a bench.
#
# ⚠️ ITS STORE DECLARES `directory: false`, AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT FACT FROM THIS LINE. This says a front of
# this box answers at that hostname. The declaration says the KERNEL's address book must NOT claim it for the
# counter's store — the kernel writes `https://<host>/account/orders/<id>` into every transactional message
# and the totem serves ONE route, so a `host` there would put an «Acompanhar o pedido» button on every
# counter receipt pointing at the totem's own 404. See `_public_url_why` in seed/box.json.
FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN=

# ── THE SEED DATASET (PRE-SEED) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The example data the one-shot `seed-demo` fills a store with. It lives in the MONOREPO
# (`instances/demo/dataset`) and is pointed at BY PATH: 40 MB of JSON naming 3.6 GB of photographs is a
# generator's output, and copying it here would put it where a human edits.
#
# HOST path (what compose mounts) and CONTAINER path (what the kernel reads). Unset the container one and
# `seed-demo` answers "nothing to seed", which is the correct state for a box that wants no example data.
#
# ⛔ THIS LINE IS THE ONE INPUT OF THIS BOX THAT IS NOT PINNED, AND ON 2026-09-03 IT WAS 166 COMMITS STALE.
# It pointed at a worktree that had been the oven two days earlier; the box came up GREEN, the admin's stock
# panel was born empty with the feature that fills it INSIDE the image, and the whole demo — 2 790 products,
# its categories, its brands — came from yesterday's checkout. A host path in a `.env` cannot age loudly on
# its own, so `bin/box-up.sh` step 0c now makes the noise: it compares the pointer of THIS directory against
# the one `forge.lock` recorded when the images were baked, and refuses the birth NAMING BOTH STAMPS. Seconds,
# before a single container starts. `node bin/dataset-provenance.mjs` asks the same question by hand.
#
# ⇒ So this value is EXPECTED to go stale, and that is now survivable. Point it at the monorepo checkout named
#   by `forge.lock` → `provenance.built_from`.
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_HOST_DIR=/path/to/forge/instances/demo/dataset
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_DIR=/app/seed-dataset
# ⚠️ THE PHOTOS ARE HYDRATED SOMEWHERE ELSE, ON PURPOSE. The seed WRITES the photo tree as it pulls, and the
# dataset above is mounted read-only because it is a shared git worktree. This points the write at a named
# volume of this box's own.
FORGE_SEED_PHOTOS_DIR=/data/seed-photos

# ── THE SEED'S PACE, ONE KNOB PER FACE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `bin/seed.mjs` paces itself below the kernel's rate limits instead of discovering them with 429s. The
# limits are PER FACE and there are three of them, so the pace is per face too — one knob for all three
# means the whole birth pays the SMALLEST of them on every call.
#
# ⚠️ MEASURED, TWICE, ON THE SAME BOX (2026-09-03): one global knob at the only value that fitted the
# tightest face (0,5/s) made the birth take **74 minutes**; the same box at 85/s took ~11 minutes and died
# on a 429 from a face that had nothing to do with the knob. The three defaults below are the kernel's own
# ceilings and no box needs to set any of them — they are here so that a box whose kernel raised a ceiling
# can say so.
#
#   FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND             commands + the internal read face, per CREDENTIAL.
#                                          Kernel ceiling: FORGE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_CREDENTIAL (6000/60s). Default 85.
#   FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND_ANONYMOUS   the public reads and the public command faces (cart, checkout,
#                                          payment…), per store+IP. Kernel ceiling: 400/60s. Default 6.
#   FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND_EXT_PUBLIC  an app's anonymous create face (/v1/ext-public — the PDP review
#                                          form), per IP. Kernel ceiling: 30/60s. Default 0.5.
FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND=
FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND_ANONYMOUS=
FORGE_SEED_RATE_PER_SECOND_EXT_PUBLIC=

# ⛔ THE ONE ABOVE IS THE SEED'S PACE, NOT THE KERNEL'S CEILING — and the tight one now has a ceiling knob of
# its own on the KERNEL side, added by `pk7/p1`:
#
#   FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_LIMIT_PER_IP     the anonymous create face's ceiling (default 30)
#   FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_WINDOW_SECONDS   the window it is counted in (default 60)
#
# ★ THEY WORK AS OF `v0.3.0-pre.48cba8b22` (04/09) — the kernel pinned in `forge.lock` now carries the slice
# that reads them; the sentence that used to stand here said they did nothing and aged the moment the images
# were baked. This
# is the pair that makes the Demo's reset cheap on BOTH sides, decided for this repository on 2026-09-03: the demo
# exists to be messed with and reset, so a visitor writing a thousand reviews is fine there and the ceiling
# that makes the seed crawl is not the one production needs. Raise the ceiling here AND the seed's pace
# above; raising only one moves nothing.
#FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_LIMIT_PER_IP=
#FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_WINDOW_SECONDS=
